<FieldGuide title="A collection, start to finish" description="What collecting fungi for science actually looks like, step by step: planning, photos and numbering in the field, uploading and drying that evening, bagging next morning, and sequencing later." eyebrow="FIELD RESEARCH TOOLKIT / START TO FINISH" heading="One collection, <em>woods to GenBank.</em>" lead="Not a gear list. This is what the work looks like over the day and a half after you pick a mushroom, and why each step is there." tourKey="field-start-to-finish" tour={tour} sources={sources} current="start">

<section id="before">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Before you go</p>
  <h2>Decide how many you can finish.</h2>
  <p>Every collection you pick commits you to an evening of uploading, labelling and loading the dehydrator, and a morning of bagging. Picking more than you can process is how specimens end up rotting in the fridge. Set a limit before you go, and leave the rest.</p>
  <p>The <a href="/investigate/collection-planner/">Collection Planner</a> works out a daily limit from your own evening time, morning time and dehydrator space.</p>
</section>

<section id="field">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">In the field · minutes per collection</p>
  <h2>Photos, number, container. In that order.</h2>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Before touching it</span><h3>Photograph it where it grew</h3><p>The habitat, the host or substrate, and the mushroom in place. See <a href="/investigate/field/photography/">Phone photos</a>.</p><div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: the mushroom in place</div></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Then</span><h3>Dig it out, whole</h3><p>Collect the whole base, and several mushrooms at different ages if there are enough. Many identifications depend on the base or on young specimens. See <a href="/investigate/field/genus-notes/">Genus notes</a>.</p></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Then</span><h3>Number it and photograph the rest</h3><p>A field slip with its number goes in the remaining photos: underside, side, base, cut section.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">2</a></sup></p><div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: the field slip in frame beside the specimen</div></li>
    <li><span class="mv-guide-when">Before the next one</span><h3>Its own container, with its slip</h3><p>A separate compartment, waxed-paper wrap or bag for each collection, with the slip inside. I use tackle boxes: one collection per compartment, and I almost never have a mix-up at this stage. Note anything you won’t see later: smell, latex colour, a bruise that’s fading, the tree it was under.</p><div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: a tackle box with one collection per compartment</div></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="evening">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">That evening</p>
  <h2>Upload, label, dehydrator. Same day.</h2>
  <p>FunDiS advises starting to dry as soon as possible after collecting.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup> Mushrooms keep changing after they’re picked: they mature, rot, and get eaten by the larvae already inside them.</p>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><h3>Post each observation</h3><p>Upload to iNaturalist with every photo for that collection. The observation number becomes the specimen number.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">2</a></sup> Add a voucher field so the record shows a specimen exists.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Optional: a spore print</h3><p>Some genera need one. Start it now, because it takes hours.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Print the labels</h3><p>Alan Rockefeller’s <a href="https://dikarya.us/labels">Herbarium Label Generator</a> prints labels for the whole day from the observation numbers.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Load the dehydrator, in order</h3><p>Each collection in its own spot, with space between them. I lay them out in the order I made the observations, starting at the 12 o’clock position on my round dehydrator, so the position on the tray tells me which observation it is. See <a href="/investigate/field/drying/">Drying &amp; storage</a>.</p><div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: a loaded tray, starting at 12 o’clock</div></li>
    <li><h3>Photograph each tray</h3><p>A quick photo of every tray, before it goes in. Some mushrooms dry down to look completely different, and the photo is how you match them up in the morning.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="morning">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Next morning</p>
  <h2>Check, bag, store.</h2>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><h3>Is it cracker dry?</h3><p>It should snap rather than bend. Drying usually takes 12–48 hours, so big or wet collections may need to stay on.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">3</a></sup></p></li>
    <li><h3>One at a time</h3><p>Take one collection off, put it in its bag with its label inside, seal it, then the next. Use last night’s tray photos to check each one. For me this takes about 30 minutes.</p><div class="mv-guide-photo">Photo to come: bagged and labelled specimens</div></li>
    <li><h3>Store it dry, and findable</h3><p>Dried fungi pick up moisture from the air, which damages DNA. Add desiccant if your storage is above 50% humidity.<sup class="mv-ref"><a href="#sources">4</a></sup> I group bags by month, by sequencing plate, or by foray event.</p></li>
    <li><h3>If you’re not sure, it doesn’t go in</h3><p>If I can’t confidently tell which collection a dried mushroom belongs to, it’s thrown away, or I make a new observation of it in the lab and note what happened. A specimen with a doubtful label does more harm than no specimen.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="later">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">Weeks to months later</p>
  <h2>Sequencing, then close the loop.</h2>
  <ol class="mv-guide-steps">
    <li><h3>Send tissue for sequencing</h3><p>A small piece of the dried specimen goes into a plate well. The <a href="/investigate/plate-tagger/">Plate Tagger</a> writes the plate onto each observation, so you can find the bag again.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Add the result to the record</h3><p>The ITS sequence goes in the “DNA Barcode ITS” field. If it doesn’t match a described species, the <a href="/investigate/provisional-finder/">Provisional Finder</a> and <a href="/investigate/its-bin-comparator/">ITS Comparator</a> help you place it.</p></li>
    <li><h3>Check nothing fell through</h3><p>The <a href="/investigate/collection-check/">Collection Check</a> lists records that are missing a step: vouchered but never sequenced, sequenced but not in GenBank, a provisional name with no specimen behind it.</p></li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section id="routine">
  <p class="mv-guide-kicker">My routine</p>
  <h2>How I keep up</h2>
  <div class="mv-guide-grid">
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Weekdays</h3><p>I collect for 1–2 hours and bring home 20–30 collections, never more than 40. About an hour that evening goes on labels and getting everything onto the dehydrator, and bagging the next morning takes about 30 minutes.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Weekends</h3><p>I collect until I drop. There’s more time at home to keep up.</p></div>
    <div class="mv-guide-card"><h3>Why a limit</h3><p>The cap on weekdays isn’t about the woods. It’s so everything I pick gets labelled, dried and bagged properly, without anything sitting in the fridge.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>Everyone works at a different speed. The <a href="/investigate/collection-planner/">Collection Planner</a> works out your own limit from your own times.</p>
  <div class="mv-guide-note"><p>A video of the full routine will replace the photo boxes on this page when it’s ready.</p></div>
</section>

</FieldGuide>
